<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · China, Tibet and Mongolia</p><h1 class="st-title">Dazhou (Zhang Shicheng)</h1><p class="st-meta">1354 CE – 1367 CE · capital Pingjiang (Suzhou)</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The state of the salt-boatman rebel Zhang Shicheng, who ruled the rich Yangtze delta from Suzhou and was Zhu Yuanzhang's last great rival in the east.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zhang-shicheng">Zhang Shicheng</a>, a salt boatman from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taizhou-jiangsu">Taizhou</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jiangsu">Jiangsu</a>, led salt workers in revolt against <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/yuan-dynasty">Yuan Dynasty</a> officials in 1353 and in 1354 proclaimed himself King of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/zhou-wu-sangui">Great Zhou</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaoyou">Gaoyou</a>. A huge Yuan army under the chancellor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toghto">Toghto</a> besieged Gaoyou, but dissolved when Toghto was dismissed.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Zhang moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suzhou">Pingjiang</a> (Suzhou) in 1356 and held the wealthy delta, with its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silk">silk</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rice">rice</a>. In 1357 he nominally submitted to the Yuan and shipped grain north by sea to the capital, but kept his own administration and in 1363 took the title King of Wu. His court attracted scholars and poets, and he taxed lightly.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hongwu-emperor">Zhu Yuanzhang</a>, having defeated <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/chen-han">Chen Han</a>, attacked Zhang's territories from 1365 and besieged <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suzhou">Suzhou</a> for ten months. The city fell in 1367; Zhang was taken to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nanjing">Nanjing</a> and hanged himself. Zhu founded the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/ming-empire">Ming Empire</a> the next year and punished Suzhou with heavy taxes.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="kingdom, 1354 CE – 1367 CE"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M22 72 V40 L36 54 L50 28 L64 54 L78 40 V72 Z"/><path d="M22 80 H78"/><circle cx="50" cy="24" r="3"/><circle cx="22" cy="36" r="2.5"/><circle cx="78" cy="36" r="2.5"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">kingdom</p><p class="st-emblem-region">China, Tibet and Mongolia</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:86.65%;width:1.20%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1354 CE – 1367 CE</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>other</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Pingjiang (Suzhou)</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Zhang Shicheng</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Middle Chinese</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/china">China</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Great Zhou, Wu (Zhang Shicheng)</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1360&amp;focus=dazhou-zhang-shicheng" aria-label="Dazhou (Zhang Shicheng) on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7399 994 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Dazhou (Zhang Shicheng) lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M7867 1005h0M7643 1242h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7719 1250h0"/><path class="map-dot-ap" d="M7719 1250h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>Location approximate: shown in present-day China. <a href="/atlas?year=1360&amp;focus=dazhou-zhang-shicheng">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
